Blood, sweat, and tears are three ways the body makes an otherwise invisible cost visible. Sweat can show the work in progress. Tears can arrive when the work is over and you finally have room to feel it. Blood can mark where something gave way. None of them tells the whole story on its own.

And releases can look like laughter, a long exhale, finally saying the thing, or putting down a responsibility you’ve carried for years. Sometimes you can see the change in what stops happening. The joke lands and you don’t brace. The old argument presents itself and you let it pass. Same cue, different next move.

That ties your two ideas together. Knowledge as a memory virus changes what an old scene means. Release is one way you find out that the old meaning has loosened its grip. You remember the same words, but this time you laugh, grieve, speak up, or simply go back to enjoying your day.

For Silent Guitar, that last one is a rest you can only hear if you know the old rhythm. You know exactly where you used to tighten up. This time there was space.